			<!-- On speed — the reframe -->
			<section class="py-[clamp(48px,6vw,88px)]">
				<div class="wrap">
					<SectionHead
						eyebrow="On speed"
						title="Fast to a draft is not fast to a deck."
					>
						An AI generator is fast — to a first draft. That’s the familiar 80/20 of generative AI:
						the demo lands in a minute, and the last mile is still yours. Then the real clock starts, and it runs long.
					</SectionHead>
					<div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-[18px] lg:grid-cols-[1fr_1fr]">
						<div class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-card p-[26px] shadow-sm">
							<h3 class="m-0 mb-3 t-body text-[18px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] text-[var(--text-heading)]">Their clock</h3>
							<p class="m-0 text-[15px] leading-[1.6] text-foreground">
								You fact-check the numbers the model invented. You strip the sameness so it doesn’t read as a template.
								You fix the export that came back with the wrong fonts. You force the whole thing back onto the brand.
								The draft arrived in a minute; the deck you can send is hours away — and the same hours come due on the
								next deck, and the one after that.
							</p>
						</div>
						<div class="rounded-xl border border-primary p-[26px] shadow-sm" style="background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 7%, transparent);">
							<h3 class="m-0 mb-3 t-body text-[18px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] text-primary">Ours</h3>
							<p class="m-0 text-[15px] leading-[1.6] text-foreground">
								Lattice front-loads the cost once. You learn the tool and you write the words — and after that, every
								render is final: on brand, correct, identical each time. No re-brand, no broken PPTX, no fact-checking the
								layout, not on this deck and not on the next fifty. Their draft is fast and the fixing never ends; ours
								starts slower and the finish comes free.
							</p>
						</div>
					</div>
					<p class="mt-8 max-w-[46ch] t-display text-[clamp(22px,2.6vw,30px)] font-bold leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.01em] text-[var(--text-heading)]">
						The artsy deck wins the demo. The deterministic deck wins the boardroom.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			<!-- What we commit to (policy) -->
